    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">April 1519</span><span class="rf-text">Cortés lands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> with 508 soldiers, 100 sailors and 14 small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1519</span><span class="rf-text">The Spaniards enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and take Moctezuma hostage</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1520</span><span class="rf-text">On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-noche-triste">La Noche Triste</a> the Spaniards flee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> causeway with heavy losses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1520</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> breaks out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and kills <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuitlahuac">Cuitlahuac</a>, who is followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1521</span><span class="rf-text">Cortés launches thirteen sloops on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> and begins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1521</span><span class="rf-text">Three Spanish forces with their allies take up positions at Tacuba, Coyohuacan and Ixtlapalapan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1521</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> surrender and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> is captured, after which the city is sacked</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1525</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> is hanged for treason during a Spanish expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="fall-of-tenochtitlan" data-v="2952a95afb" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The three camps and the sloops take their places</b> Cortés sent Alvarado to Tacuba, Olid to Coyohuacan and Sandoval to Iztapalapa, each with Spanish troops and about 8,000 allies, and kept the thirteen sloops under his own command on the lake. The Mexica held the city with their canoes.</li><li><b>Alvarado and Olid march to Chapultepec</b> Alvarado and Olid marched first towards Chapultepec to cut the Mexica off from their water supply. Springs there fed much of the city by aqueduct, and the rest of its water came in by canoe.</li><li><b>Alvarado and Olid are pushed back at Tlacopan</b> Alvarado and Olid then tried to bring their forces over the causeway at Tlacopan. The Mexica drove them back and halted the assault with a hard-fought counterattack by land and by canoe.</li><li><b>The sloops meet the canoes on the lake</b> Cortés launched the thirteen sloops from Texcoco and met more than a thousand canoes. A favourable breeze let him overturn many of them and kill or capture many of their crews.</li><li><b>A gap is opened and the sloops are shared out</b> After the first battle on the lake Cortés camped with Olid's forces. He opened a gap in the causeway for the sloops and gave four to Alvarado, six to Olid and two to Sandoval, so that the canoes could no longer attack from the lake side.</li><li><b>The camps hold the causeways and creep forward</b> The Spaniards stayed on the causeways at night, since the Mexica occupied any bridge they left. They razed the districts they took and used the rubble to fill the canals and gaps, which let them advance closer to the city.</li><li><b>The Mexica ambush two Spanish launches</b> The Mexica set an ambush with thirty pirogues in water where they had fixed impaling stakes. They captured two Spanish launches and killed Captains Juan de la Portilla and Pedro Barba.</li><li><b>The Spaniards spring a counter-ambush</b> After capturing two chieftains, Cortés learned of a plot to ambush his launches with forty pirogues. He met it with six launches, killing many warriors and taking many prisoners, and the Mexica laid no more ambushes.</li><li><b>Cuauhtemoc attacks all three camps</b> Cuauhtemoc attacked all three Spanish camps at once with his entire army. The Spanish kept their camps on the causeways.</li><li><b>Alvarado's charge on the Tacuba Causeway is cut off</b> Alvarado charged his cavalry across a gap in the Tacuba Causeway. As he came out on the other side with the infantry behind, Aztec canoes filled the gap. He was wounded along with eight men, and five of his men were captured.</li><li><b>Cortés pushes on to the market and is thrown back</b> Cortés pushed forward towards the Mexican market square but did not fill in a channel as he advanced. The Mexica counter-attacked, Cortés was wounded and almost captured, and sixty-five Spanish soldiers were taken alive.</li><li><b>Alvarado reaches Tlatelolco first</b> Cortés ordered all three camps to advance together on the Tlatelolco marketplace. Alvarado's company arrived first, and Gutierrez de Badajoz climbed the Huichilopotzli temple, set it on fire and planted Spanish banners.</li><li><b>Cortés's and Sandoval's men join Alvarado</b> The men of Cortés and Sandoval joined Alvarado's company after four more days of fighting. The Spaniards and their allies went on into the city, and the Mexica, though they inflicted heavy casualties, could not halt them.</li><li><b>Sandoval attacks and the sloops intercept the canoes</b> Cortés ordered Sandoval to attack the part of the city where Cuauhtemoc had retreated. Hundreds of canoes fled the city and Cortés sent the sloops to intercept them, and the Mexica forces were destroyed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In April 1519 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> with 508 soldiers, 100 sailors and 14 small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a>, the Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, had revoked his mandate before he sailed, so Cortés created a local government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and had himself elected its magistrate, answerable in theory only to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He landed on the borders of Cempoala, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> with many grievances against them, and met other polities that resented Aztec rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> gave him two battles in a day and one at night before they began to consider his offers of peace; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xicotencatl-the-elder">Xicotencatl the Elder</a> wanted an alliance against the Aztecs, which was also Cortés's aim.</p><p>In November 1519 the Spaniards entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and were received amicably by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. On 14 November they took him hostage as a safety measure, because they were so outnumbered. Why Moctezuma cooperated is uncertain. Cortés knew little of how the Aztecs were ruled, and as Moctezuma obeyed his orders his authority slipped.</p><p>Cortés left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> in the city with 80 soldiers and marched to the coast against the force Velázquez had sent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a>, which he defeated and absorbed. At the festival of Toxcatl Alvarado ordered the gates of the patio closed and killed many thousands of Aztec nobles, warriors and priests. The massacre turned all the Aztecs against the Spanish and undermined Moctezuma's authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl.jpg" alt="Ahuitzotl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahuitzotl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For the siege Cortés had 84 horsemen, 194 crossbowmen and arquebusiers and 650 Spanish foot soldiers, besides thirteen sloops built under his master shipbuilder, Martín López, and launched at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>. Each carried 25 men: 12 oarsmen, 12 crossbowmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, and a captain. He also had 20,000 warriors from Tlaxcala, Huexotzinco and Cholula, led by Xicotencatl II and Chichimecatecuhtli. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> were the largest group of Indigenous allies, but the Huexotzinco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalca">Chalca</a>, Tetzcocans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanecs</a> and others had all been subjugated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> too. The Tlaxcalans had required heavy concessions, among them a share of the spoils and exemption from future tribute.</p><p>Cortés divided his army in three. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Alvarado</a> took 30 horsemen, 18 crossbowmen and arquebusiers, 150 foot and 8,000 Tlaxcalans to Tacuba. Cristóbal de Olid took 30 horsemen, 20 crossbowmen and arquebusiers, 175 foot and 8,000 Tlaxcalans to Coyohuacan. Gonzalo de Sandoval took 24 horsemen, 14 arquebusiers and crossbowmen, 150 foot and 8,000 warriors from Chalco and Huexotzinco to Ixtlapalapan. Cortés took the sloops. All were in place on 22 May.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> now had a new tlatoani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, enthroned in February 1521 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuitlahuac">Cuitlahuac</a>. Their tactics were excellent: canoes, flat roofs with prepared missiles, dropping into the lake when cornered, and destroying bridges. They also fixed hidden stakes in the lake bottom. They stayed in the city, near its supplies, with the causeways the only way in.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Alvarado</a> and Olid first marched to Chapultepec to cut the springs that supplied much of the city's water by aqueduct. Their attempt on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> causeway was pushed back. Cortés launched his sloops against more than a thousand canoes, and a favourable breeze let him overturn many of them. He then opened a gap in the causeways so the sloops could help defend his forces from both sides, and shared them out: four to Alvarado, six to Olid and two to Sandoval. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> occupied each abandoned bridge and barricade when the Spaniards withdrew at night, so Cortés kept his men on the causeways overnight. Two sloops were lost in an ambush at the stakes.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> attacked all three camps at once on the feast of St John. Alvarado was wounded and five of his men were captured. In the advance on the market square, the Battle of Colhuacatonco, Cortés neglected to fill a channel, was wounded and nearly captured, and 65 Spaniards were taken alive. The Mexica sacrificed prisoners on the temple in sight of the camps, and Bernal Díaz describes the sacrifice and the eating of the dead. Of 24,000 allies only 200 stayed in the camps for a time; after twelve days others returned. Cortés razed each neighbourhood he took and filled the canals with the rubble.</p><p>Food and water were cut off, and famine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and brackish water spread dysentery in the city. All three camps advanced on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> market. Alvarado's men reached it first and fired the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>. Cuauhtémoc tried to flee in fifty pirogues and was captured by Sandoval's launches.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> surrendered on 13 August 1521. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> was brought before Cortés, who demanded the gold lost on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-noche-triste">La Noche Triste</a>. Cuauhtémoc and the lord of Tacuba were tortured by having their feet burned with oil. Little gold remained, since a fifth had been sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and another part kept by Cortés. Cuauhtémoc stayed the titular leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> under Cortés until he was hanged for treason in 1525.</p><p>One estimate puts the dead of the whole campaign, warriors and civilians, at 100,000 to 240,000. As many as 40,000 bodies were floating in the canals or awaiting burial after the siege, and at least 40,000 civilians were killed or captured. Almost all the nobility were dead, and the survivors were mostly young women and very young children, who marched out of the city over three days.</p><p>Cortés's Indigenous allies went on killing after the surrender and looted the city, entering houses, raping and killing women and stabbing children. One source claims 6,000 were massacred in Ixtapalapa alone. Charles Robinson wrote that centuries of hate and the nature of Mesoamerican warfare combined in a violence that appalled Cortés himself. Cortés wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> that his men had more trouble preventing the killing than fighting, that the allies outnumbered the Spaniards (more than 150,000 against some nine hundred), and that he could not prevent more than fifteen thousand being killed and sacrificed that day.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florentine-codex.jpg" alt="Florentine Codex" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_florentino_51_9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernardino de Sahagún, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The principal accounts are by participants and by people who talked to them. Cortés wrote letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, which explain and defend his conduct to the Crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a>, a lieutenant of Cortés, gave a soldier's account, and Francisco López de Gómara was Cortés's chaplain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a> preserves the view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, including the effects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, and the Aztec codices depict the disease. Accounts of the first meeting of Cortés and Moctezuma differ on whether Cortés touched him, and Doña Marina and Gerónimo de Aguilar translated in two steps, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chontal-maya">Chontal Maya</a> to Spanish, which left room for misunderstanding.</p><p>Several points are disputed. The Spanish said Moctezuma was stoned by his own people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> later said the Spanish murdered him. The belief that the Aztecs took Cortés for the returning god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a> is now thought a myth of the conquerors, and most scholars hold that Moctezuma's inner circle did not believe it. Spanish sources say a slave from Narváez's force, Francisco de Eguía, carried smallpox into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>Losses are given very differently. For the Spaniards, 100 or more were lost in the siege, though some reports say as few as forty. About 1,800 Spaniards are estimated to have died from all causes in the two-year campaign, and other sources put the dead of 1519 to 1521 at about 860 Spaniards and 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>. The estimate of 100,000 to 240,000 dead for the Mexica covers the whole campaign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> was an important event in the Spanish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and a critical stage in the Spanish colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The siege led directly to the end of the Aztec civilisation and to the sacking and violence against the survivors. Because of the slaughter after the campaign and the destruction of Aztec culture, Israel Charney, John C. Cox and Norman Naimark consider the siege a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><p>Cortés's Indigenous allies were indispensable to his success, and they may have numbered as many as 200,000 over the three years of the conquest. Bernal Díaz especially credited La Malinche with the victory. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> the siege was a chance to end a hundred years in which they had been forced to hand over an annual quota of young men and women to be sacrificed and eaten at the Great Pyramid of Tenochtitlan. Spanish authorities later disowned the treaty Cortés had made with the Tlaxcalans.</p><p>Cortés's conduct was examined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1529, and no action was taken against him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic was a central factor: the Mexica lost approximately half their population to smallpox and other diseases, and the disease killed an estimated forty percent of the native population in the area within a year.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In 1521 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, with a few hundred Spaniards, thirteen sloops and tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, cut off its food and water and took it on 13 August. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> and famine had weakened the city, and the sack that followed killed thousands more.</em></p>

